<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Luis Gálvez Rodríguez de Arias</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1864">1864</a>–<a href="/years/1935">1935</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Luis Gálvez Rodríguez de Arias was a Spanish journalist and adventurer in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amazon">Amazon</a>. On 14 July 1899, backed by Brazilian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rubber">rubber</a> interests in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acre">Acre</a>, then Bolivian territory, he proclaimed the Independent State of Acre at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puerto-alonso">Puerto Alonso</a> and made himself president. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>, then still recognising Bolivian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a>, sent forces that removed him in 1900. Acre passed to Brazil by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-petropolis">Treaty of Petrópolis</a> in 1903.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/luis-galvez-rodriguez-de-arias.jpg" alt="Luis Gálvez Rodríguez de Arias" width="244" height="376" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Martimssilveira · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Luis-Galvez-Caras-Caretas-1902.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
